#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20159e8-8f86-5bf8-b87e-3eb990b819f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Not a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "853b8f11-09d0-5d52-bf7d-40d4f572e271" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(t) = \\frac{1}{4}(\\cos (2t) - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080581} {:answer/id #uuid "9edc1fa0-3840-58e3-a86e-02e4168b5fce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(t) = \\frac{1}{4}(\\sin (2t) - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080582} {:answer/id #uuid "f6bcd9af-c1a7-55d3-93ad-e47d76531ffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(t) = \\frac{1}{4}\\sin (2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080583} {:answer/id #uuid "04882f00-6cee-5b09-9b6b-aeefe3d07101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(t) = \\frac{1}{2}\\sin (2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080584} {:answer/id #uuid "5731ec29-802c-5212-8953-9e422705da41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(t) = \\frac{1}{2}\\cos (2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6bcd9af-c1a7-55d3-93ad-e47d76531ffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(t) = \\frac{1}{4}\\sin (2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080583} :answer-field/id #uuid "b290d70a-81eb-5aad-8a56-8b792d09ece7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66781b23-5a65-5eb8-b6bb-bf173b8aafe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-19486" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. Given that it is at rest when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, find the velocity (in $\\text{m/s}$)  of the particle at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20159e8-8f86-5bf8-b87e-3eb990b819f1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Not a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "909c1bf0-7a2d-5e71-9443-8e878901ad01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.25\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348341} {:answer/id #uuid "e172556b-a8e8-5b18-b447-8c1e26b8c58f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348342} {:answer/id #uuid "43319795-a130-5f4a-91ef-e799d91f4c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348343} {:answer/id #uuid "990d214e-6a5e-55e5-9306-11fef3b5ddc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348344} {:answer/id #uuid "11a56cdf-8f21-555b-80a9-2084e339cf20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.625\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43319795-a130-5f4a-91ef-e799d91f4c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348343} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd9d928a-ed3b-5f20-aa17-e6eb6628bba2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "66dc2a76-d4e2-534c-99d2-6f4373844378" :question/math-academy-id "q-19489" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t),$ where $a(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ and $t$ is measured in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4(-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{4}{5}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot 0)+C \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+C \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot 1+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =-0.8\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $0.8\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee2920dc-93e1-5595-a751-62ad5e4f3035" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Given By a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa0f6412-9d9a-5e5f-b235-30bf39102742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t^7+3t^4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289918} {:answer/id #uuid "438fe4b3-62f1-5533-a20c-d8669ccf09e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t^7+3t^4+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "438fe4b3-62f1-5533-a20c-d8669ccf09e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t^7+3t^4+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289919} :answer-field/id #uuid "a309ad20-6987-57d0-b45b-abe948c42042" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e1638b5-7055-56e8-8524-6658a022f221" :question/math-academy-id "q-350978" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=14{t}^{6}+12{t}^{3},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. Find the velocity $v(t)$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of the particle at time $t$ given that the particle has velocity $v=310\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (14{t}^{6}+12{t}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{7}+3{t}^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(2)=310.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}310 & =2({2}^{7})+3({2}^{4})+C \\\\ 310 & =304+C \\\\ C & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{7}+3{t}^{4}+6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee2920dc-93e1-5595-a751-62ad5e4f3035" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Given By a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ade58f70-ab02-5935-ab25-68c6237e338c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t^5+2t^3-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ade58f70-ab02-5935-ab25-68c6237e338c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t^5+2t^3-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348348} :answer-field/id #uuid "15b03d7c-914a-5bf8-9ca9-acfd2e7f2e87" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc7a0ebd-53d2-5ffe-8966-1b20839d3b60" :question/math-academy-id "q-351004" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=15{t}^{4}+6{t}^{2},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. Find the velocity $v(t)$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of the particle at time $t$ given that the particle has velocity $v=100\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (15{t}^{4}+6{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{5}+2{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(2)=100.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =3({2}^{5})+2({2}^{3})+C \\\\ 100 & =96+16+C \\\\ 100 & =112+C \\\\ C & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=3{t}^{5}+2{t}^{3}-12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee2920dc-93e1-5595-a751-62ad5e4f3035" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Given By a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bfc1bda-5eff-5a6f-b371-ab5883fc7a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082329} {:answer/id #uuid "cb76cb83-825b-5d0d-be09-9bfb4563099c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082330} {:answer/id #uuid "d0045836-72f5-5b20-a3a4-bbfd93f1daf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082331} {:answer/id #uuid "50ffc313-9571-553c-b356-7dae55d432ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082332} {:answer/id #uuid "7b99555a-846b-5a82-ba1b-a2a1f5c28fde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0045836-72f5-5b20-a3a4-bbfd93f1daf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082331} :answer-field/id #uuid "a34c9314-d67c-5e55-b802-bfbf18eee36a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fdcc1478-03c8-5a05-bbb2-e650f412de86" :question/math-academy-id "q-7112" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=2t-9{t}^{2}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-9{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-3{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=-2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =(1)^{2}-3(1)^{3}+C \\\\ -2 & =1-3+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}-3{t}^{3}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=2\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =(2)^{2}-3(2)^{3} \\\\  & =4-24 \\\\  & =-20\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $20\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}]}
